/// What runs when a value dies (§6.5). Implementing it makes the type move-only, and it
/// becomes the type's C++ destructor — which is why it may only be implemented for a
/// struct, in the struct's own module, for every instance at once (§6.1).
pub trait Drop {
    fn drop(&mut self)
}

/// A type whose value can be duplicated with `a.clone()` (§6.3, D-0106). Plain data — no
/// `drop`, no `own` field, no `clone` of its own, every field plain — is `Clone` without an
/// implementation, and copies by itself. Implementing it is how a type that owns something
/// becomes cloneable: `clone` runs on a value at its defaults, and fills it in from `other`
/// (§6.6). `impl<T: Clone> Clone for List<T>` makes `List<f32>` cloneable and leaves
/// `List<File>` move-only (§8.2, §18.26).
pub trait Clone {
    fn clone(&mut self, other: &Self)
}
